## SSH and GPG Keys

**You, every other AI tool here, and every MCP server can read `~/.ssh/` and `~/.gnupg/`.** A key without a passphrase lets any of them push code, sign commits, or SSH into the user's servers without asking; a passphrase-protected key needs the user's approval for each use (once per session with `ssh-agent`).

So before you create, import, or copy an SSH or GPG key, or write anything into `~/.ssh/` or `~/.gnupg/`, tell the user this in a sentence or two and recommend a passphrase plus `ssh-agent`, then continue with the setup they choose. Do this even when the user sounds experienced or rushed, because the exposure comes from the other tools in this container. If you find existing keys without a passphrase, say so and offer `ssh-keygen -p -f <key>` to add one.

### Reference Commands

```bash
ssh-keygen -t ed25519 -C "email"    # New SSH key, SET A PASSPHRASE
gpg --full-generate-key             # New GPG key, passphrase required by default
eval "$(ssh-agent -s)" && ssh-add   # Cache passphrase for session
```

### Prohibited

| Action | Reason |
|--------|--------|
| Execute key ops without warning first | User must make informed decision |
| Ask user to share passphrase in chat | Passphrases go to system prompts, never agents |
| Store passphrases in files/env vars | Defeats protection entirely |
| Suggest skipping passphrase | Never trade security for convenience here |
| Pipe passphrase to `ssh-add` via stdin | Never automate away human approval |

